(defun most-common (list)
    "Returns the most common item in list, breaking ties arbitrarily"
    (let ((reduced-elts (mapcar (lambda (elt) (list elt (count elt list :test #'equalp)))
                    (remove-duplicates list :test #'equalp))))
    (first (first (sort reduced-elts #'> :key #'second)))))


(defun k-nearest-neighbor (examples new-example &key (k 1) (measure 'count))
    "Given a LIST of EXAMPLES, each of the form (list-of-data class), and a NEW-EXAMPLE
    of the same form, determines the K EXAMPLES which most closely resemble the NEW-EXAMPLE,
    then returns the most common class among them.  The MEASURE used to compute similarity
    can be provided.  Note that the CLASS is a LIST, like (0.9), not 0.9."

    ;;;; IMPLEMENT ME
    (let* ((neighbor-pair (mapcar ;builds a pair when given the two lists; the distance and the class
                        (lambda (example) ;lambda takes in the example, compares it, and pairs it with the class
                            (list 
                                (difference (first new-example) (first example) :measure measure)
                                (second example))) 
                        examples))

            (sorted (sort neighbor-pair #'< :key #'first)) ;sort the pairs by the differences
            (neighbors (subseq sorted 0 k))) ;gets the first k items after sort
        (most-common (mapcar #'second neighbors)) ;gets the most common from the k items
    )
)
#|
Function Report:
Getting the mapcar syntax right took a while and a bit of googling, but eventually I got it to work.
First I use the let*, which allows me to use neighbor-pair later in the let expression.
The mapcar builds lists with the first element being the distance between the two examples and
the second element being the class. Afterwards the pairs are sorted by lowest distance and stored
in sorted, and then the k closest neighbors are stored in neighbors. Finally I use the most-common
function provided to find the majority of the classes of the k closest neighbors, and that class is 
returned as the class of new-example.
|#


(defun generalization (training-examples test-examples &key (k 1) (measure 'count))
    "Given a list of TRAINING EXAMPLES, each of the form (list-of-data class),
    and a set of TEST EXAMPLES, each of the same form, returns the percentage of TEST
    EXAMPLES correctly classified when passed to K-NEAREST-NEIGHBOR with the TRAINING
    EXAMPLES.  Note that the CLASS is a LIST, like (0.9), not 0.9."

    (let ((num-correct 0)) ;counter
        (dolist (test test-examples)  ;loop through tests
            (if (equalp (k-nearest-neighbor training-examples test :k k :measure measure) (second test)) ;check test compared to training
                (incf num-correct)
            )
        )
        (* (/ num-correct (length test-examples)) 100) ;calc percentage
    )
)
#|
Function Report:
First I setup a counter that keeps track of how many times the k-nearest-neighbor gets the class right.
Then I loop through the test-examples for each one I use my k-nearest-neighbor if it returns the same class as
stored in the test case. If so I increment the counter. After going through all of the test cases I calculate the percentage
and it is returned, usually as a fraction.
|#


